<script>(function(){var P=[["Santería","/religion/santeria","Religion","c. 1880 CE – present","Santería, also called Regla de Ocha, Regla Lucumí or Lucumí, is an Afro-Cuban religion that arose in the late 19th century from Yoruba religion, Catholicism and Spiritism, and is known for its oricha, its initiations and its drumming ceremonies."],["Capitalism","/ideology/capitalism","Ideology","c. 1500 CE – present","Capitalism is an economic system based on private ownership of the means of production and its use for profit; emerging from 16th-century English agriculture and European mercantilism, it became dominant with the Industrial Revolution and spread worldwide."],["Anti-apartheid movement","/movement/anti-apartheid-movement","Movement","1948 CE – 1994 CE","The struggle inside South Africa and across the world against apartheid, the system of racial segregation and white minority rule imposed from 1948, which ended with the first democratic election in 1994."],["Moai","/work/moai","Great work","c. 1250 CE – present","The moai are monolithic human figures carved from stone by the Rapa Nui people on Rapa Nui (Easter Island) in eastern Polynesia, mostly between 1250 and 1500, and chiefly regarded as the living faces of deified ancestors."],["Feudalism","/government/feudalism","Form of government","c. 800 CE – 1789 CE","Feudalism was the medieval European order in which lords granted fiefs to vassals for military service and loyalty, leaving public power divided among landholders; historians now debate whether the term fits."],["Canaanite religion","/religion/canaanite-religion","Religion","c. 3000 BCE – c. 400 CE","Canaanite religion was the polytheistic faith of the peoples of Canaan in the southern Levant during about three millennia BCE, its pantheon headed by El and Asherah, and is known chiefly from the Ugaritic texts and the Hebrew Bible."],["Hindutva","/ideology/hindutva","Ideology","1923 CE – present","Hindutva is a Hindu nationalist ideology, formulated by Vinayak Damodar Savarkar in Essentials of Hindutva in 1923, that grounds Indian nationhood in a shared Hindu culture and guides the RSS, the VHP and the ruling Bharatiya Janata Party."],["Protestant Reformation","/movement/protestant-reformation","Movement","1517 CE – 1648 CE","The religious movement, begun by Martin Luther in 1517, that broke the unity of Western Christianity, created the Lutheran, Reformed, Anglican and Anabaptist churches and reshaped European politics and society."],["Petra","/work/petra","Great work","c. 400 BCE – present","Petra is the rock-cut city in southern Jordan that the Nabataeans settled in the 4th century BCE and made their capital, a trading hub on the incense routes known for its carved sandstone façades and water systems."],["City-state","/government/city-state","Form of government","c. 3500 BCE – present","A city-state is a single city that governs itself and its surrounding countryside; from Sumer, Greece and Phoenicia to Venice and the Hanseatic towns it nurtured citizenship, trade and republican government."],["Catharism","/religion/catharism","Religion","c. 1143 CE – c. 1350 CE","Catharism was a Christian dualist movement of Occitania and northern Italy between the 12th and 14th centuries, whose Good Christians were attacked by the Albigensian Crusade and the Inquisition; scholars still debate how far it existed as an organised religion."],["Ba'athism","/ideology/baathism","Ideology","1940 CE – present","Ba'athism is an Arab nationalist ideology of a unified Arab state ruled by a vanguard party under revolutionary socialism; it arose in Syria in the 1940s and shaped the one-party regimes of Syria and Iraq."],["Taiping Rebellion","/movement/taiping-rebellion","Movement","1850 CE – 1864 CE","The Taiping Rebellion was a civil war of 1850–1864 in which the Taiping Heavenly Kingdom of Hong Xiuquan, seeking to overthrow the Qing dynasty and remake Chinese society and religion, ruled much of southern China from Nanjing before its defeat."],["Tōdai-ji","/work/todai-ji","Great work","738 CE – present","Tōdai-ji, the Eastern Great Temple at Nara in Japan, was founded in 738 and opened in 752 under Emperor Shōmu; it houses the Great Buddha of Vairocana and was the central temple of Nara-period Buddhism."],["Aristocracy","/government/aristocracy","Form of government","c. 750 BCE – c. 1918 CE","Rule by a small privileged class claiming to be the best fitted to govern, in practice usually a hereditary nobility, from the early Greek city-states to the upper houses of Europe’s monarchies."],["Mormonism","/religion/mormonism","Religion","c. 1820 CE – present","Mormonism is the theology and religious tradition of the Latter Day Saint movement, a Restorationist Christian movement started by Joseph Smith in Western New York in the 1820s and 1830s and known for the Book of Mormon."],["Three Principles of the People","/ideology/three-principles-of-the-people","Ideology","1905 CE – present","The Three Principles of the People are the nationalist, republican, anti-imperialist ideology of Sun Yat-sen, made of nationalism, democracy and people's welfare; set out in 1905, they became the official ideology of the Kuomintang's state."],["Zionism","/movement/zionism","Movement","1882 CE – present","Zionism is a nationalist movement that emerged in late 19th-century Europe to establish a Jewish homeland in Palestine, roughly the Land of Israel of Judaism; it was the main force behind the creation of the State of Israel in 1948."],["Golden Temple","/work/golden-temple","Great work","1581 CE – present","The Golden Temple, or Harmandir Sahib, is the gurdwara at Amritsar in Punjab that Sikhs hold to be their holiest site, begun by Guru Arjan in 1581 around a pool dug by his predecessors and gilded under Ranjit Singh."],["Chiefdom","/government/chiefdom","Form of government","c. 5000 BCE – present","A chiefdom unites several villages under a hereditary paramount chief who rules through kinship, ritual and tribute rather than officials, a form that governed peoples from ancient Mesopotamia to Hawaii and Africa."],["Hawaiian religion","/religion/hawaiian-religion","Religion","c. 800 CE – present","Hawaiian religion, also known as the kapu system, comprises the Indigenous beliefs and practices of Native Hawaiians: a polytheistic tradition with Kāne, Kū, Lono and Kanaloa among its chief gods, brought by Polynesian settlers between 500 and 1300 CE."],["Pan-Slavism","/ideology/pan-slavism","Ideology","c. 1848 CE – present","Pan-Slavism is a political ideology that emphasised the unity and integrity of the Slavic peoples; it arose in the mid-19th century from Romantic nationalism and shaped the Prague congress of 1848, Russian Balkan policy and the Yugoslav idea."],["Pan-Africanism","/movement/pan-africanism","Movement","1900 CE – present","A movement for the unity, dignity and freedom of African peoples at home and in the diaspora, from the London conference of 1900 through the struggle against colonial rule to the African Union."],["Records of the Grand Historian","/work/records-of-the-grand-historian","Great work","c. 110 BCE – present","The Records of the Grand Historian, or Shiji, is a Chinese history written in the late 2nd and early 1st centuries BCE by Sima Qian, building on his father's work, and the first of the Twenty-Four Histories of imperial China."],["Colonial rule","/government/colonial-rule","Form of government","1415 CE – present","Colonial rule is government of a territory and its people by a distant state that treats them as subjects rather than citizens; from 1415 to the 20th century it carried European power across most of the world."],["Sikhism","/religion/sikhism","Religion","c. 1499 CE – present","The faith founded by Guru Nanak in the Punjab around 1500 and shaped by nine Gurus after him: devotion to one God, honest work, sharing and equality, with the Guru Granth Sahib as its living Guru."],["Utilitarianism","/ideology/utilitarianism","Ideology","c. 1780 CE – present","Utilitarianism is a family of ethical theories holding that actions should maximise happiness and well-being for all affected; it took shape in 18th-century England with Jeremy Bentham and has been applied to economics, law, poverty and animal ethics."],["American Indian Movement","/movement/american-indian-movement","Movement","1968 CE – present","The American Indian Movement (AIM) is a grassroots movement founded in Minneapolis in July 1968 to address poverty, discrimination and police brutality against American Indians, and later treaty rights, land and the preservation of Indigenous cultures."],["Muqaddimah","/work/muqaddimah","Great work","1377 CE – present","The Muqaddimah is a 14th-century Arabic treatise on historiography, sociology and the philosophy of history by Ibn Khaldun, written in 1377 as the introduction to his world history, the Kitab al-ʿIbar, and read as a work in its own right."],["Direct democracy","/government/direct-democracy","Form of government","508 BCE – present","Government in which the citizens themselves decide laws and policies, in an open assembly or by popular vote, instead of through elected representatives: practised in classical Athens and in Switzerland today."],["Buddhism","/religion/buddhism","Religion","c. 500 BCE – present","A path of ethics, meditation and insight taught by Siddhartha Gautama, the Buddha, in north-east India in the 5th century BCE, which spread across Asia in its Theravada, Mahayana and Vajrayana forms."],["Anarchism","/ideology/anarchism","Ideology","c. 1840 CE – present","Anarchism seeks to abolish the state, capitalism and all institutions of authority, coercion and hierarchy; it arose in 19th-century Europe with Proudhon, Bakunin and Kropotkin and flourished in workers’ struggles worldwide until the Spanish Civil War."],["Meiji Restoration","/movement/meiji-restoration","Movement","1853 CE – 1889 CE","The Meiji Restoration was the political upheaval that ended the Tokugawa shogunate and restored power to the Emperor of Japan in 1868, then abolished the domains and the samurai class and set Japan on a course of Westernisation and industrialisation."],["Book of the Dead","/work/book-of-the-dead","Great work","c. 1700 BCE – present","The Book of the Dead is an ancient Egyptian funerary text of magic spells, written mostly on papyrus from the beginning of the New Kingdom to around 50 BCE, meant to guide the dead through the underworld into the afterlife."],["Federation","/government/federation","Form of government","1789 CE – present","A state whose constitution divides power between a central government and self-governing member states, neither able to abolish the other: pioneered by the United States in 1789, now home to some 40 per cent of humanity."],["Judaism","/religion/judaism","Religion","c. 1000 BCE – present","The religion of the Jewish people, rooted in the covenant between the one God and Israel described in the Hebrew Bible, which survived exile and dispersion for two and a half thousand years through the Torah, the synagogue and the rabbinic tradition."],["Manifest destiny","/ideology/manifest-destiny","Ideology","1845 CE – c. 1900 CE","Manifest destiny was the belief, named in 1845, that American settlers were destined to expand westward across North America; it shaped the annexation of Texas, the Oregon and Mexican cessions and the removal of Native peoples."],["Labour movement","/movement/labour-movement","Movement","c. 1800 CE – present","The organised effort of wage-earners, through trade unions, cooperatives and parties of their own, to win better pay, shorter hours, safer work and a voice in politics, born with industry around 1800."],["Dresden Codex","/work/dresden-codex","Great work","c. 1200 CE – present","The Dresden Codex is a Maya screenfold book of bark paper from the Yucatán Peninsula, probably from the Chichen Itza region, dated to the 11th or 12th century, holding astronomical tables, ritual schedules and medicine, and now kept in Dresden."],["Republic","/government/republic","Form of government","c. 509 BCE – present","Government without a monarch, in which the state is the public’s affair, run by elected magistrates and councils under law: born in ancient Rome, revived in medieval Italy and the Netherlands, now the usual form."],["Chinese folk religion","/religion/chinese-folk-religion","Religion","c. 1250 BCE – present","Chinese folk religion is the traditional religion of the Han Chinese and their diaspora: the worship of gods, immortals and ancestors, rooted in ancient divination and blended with Buddhist, Confucian and Taoist teachings by the Song dynasty."],["Conservatism","/ideology/conservatism","Ideology","c. 1790 CE – present","Conservatism is a cultural, social and political philosophy that seeks to preserve traditional institutions, customs and values; it took shape in Edmund Burke’s opposition to the French Revolution in the 1790s and became one of the three major modern ideologies."],["Luddites","/movement/luddites","Movement","1811 CE – 1816 CE","The Luddites were English textile workers who, between 1811 and 1816, broke machines in organised raids in the Midlands, Yorkshire and the North West, protesting over pay, working conditions, child labour and the quality of output."],["Romance of the Three Kingdoms","/work/romance-of-the-three-kingdoms","Great work","1522 CE – present","Romance of the Three Kingdoms is a 14th-century Chinese historical novel attributed to Luo Guanzhong, covering the decline of the Han dynasty to the reunification of China under the Western Jin in 280, in 120 chapters."],["Parliamentary democracy","/government/parliamentary-democracy","Form of government","c. 1721 CE – present","Democracy in which the government is formed from an elected parliament and stays in office only while it keeps parliament’s confidence: born in 18th-century Britain, now the usual form in Europe and the Commonwealth."],["Hinduism","/religion/hinduism","Religion","c. 1500 BCE – present","The family of traditions that grew from the Vedic religion of ancient India, with its many gods, its teachings of dharma, karma and release, and a literature from the Vedas to the epics, now the faith of about 1.2 billion people."],["Christian democracy","/ideology/christian-democracy","Ideology","c. 1891 CE – present","Christian democracy is an ideology inspired by Christian social teaching, drawn mainly from Catholic social thought and neo-Calvinism, which arose in 19th-century Europe and shaped postwar welfare states, social market economies and the European Union."],["Underground Railroad","/movement/underground-railroad","Movement","c. 1780 CE – 1863 CE","The Underground Railroad was a network of secret routes and safe houses, organised from the 1780s and run chiefly by free and enslaved African Americans, that helped people escape slavery to free states, Canada, Mexico and the Caribbean."],["Great Zimbabwe","/work/great-zimbabwe","Great work","c. 1000 CE – present","Great Zimbabwe is the stone-walled city near Masvingo built by ancestral Shona people from the 11th to the 15th century, capital of the Kingdom of Great Zimbabwe and the largest of the many 'houses of stone' of southern Africa."],["Nomadic confederation","/government/nomadic-confederation","Form of government","209 BCE – 1757 CE","A nomadic confederation joins herding tribes under one ruling clan and its khan; from the Xiongnu to the Mongols such steppe powers built the largest land empires and drew tribute from China and Constantinople."],["Mandaeism","/religion/mandaeism","Religion","c. 100 CE – present","Mandaeism is a Gnostic, monotheistic and ethnic religion of the Mandaeans, centred on the Great Life, Hayyi Rabbi, and on John the Baptist, practised mainly in southern Iraq and Khuzestan and known for its frequent baptisms in flowing water."],["Republicanism","/ideology/republicanism","Ideology","c. 500 BCE – present","Republicanism is a political ideology which holds that sovereignty resides in the people and their elected representatives rather than a hereditary monarch; rooted in republican Rome and revived in the Italian city-states, it shaped the American and French Revolutions."],["Civil rights movement","/movement/civil-rights-movement","Movement","1954 CE – 1968 CE","The movement in the United States, led chiefly by African Americans in the South, whose lawsuits and non-violent protests ended legal segregation and won federal protection of voting rights between 1954 and 1968."],["United States Declaration of Independence","/work/united-states-declaration-of-independence","Great work","1776 CE – present","The Declaration of Independence is the founding document of the United States, adopted by the Second Continental Congress in Philadelphia on 4 July 1776, explaining why the Thirteen Colonies regarded themselves as independent states no longer under British rule."],["Theocracy","/government/theocracy","Form of government","c. 515 BCE – present","Government in the name of God or the gods, by priests, a sacred ruler or religious law; the word was coined for ancient Judea, and the Papal States, Tibet under the Dalai Lamas and Iran are classic examples."],["Tengrism","/religion/tengrism","Religion","c. 350 BCE – present","Tengrism is a belief system of the Eurasian steppes, based on shamanism and animism and centred on the sky god Tengri, recorded among the Xiongnu from the 4th century BCE, the faith of Turkic and Mongol empires and revived today."],["Marxism–Leninism","/ideology/marxism-leninism","Ideology","c. 1924 CE – present","Marxism–Leninism is a communist ideology built on Marx and Lenin and formulated under Stalin in the Soviet Union in the 1920s; it became the state ideology of the Soviet Union and of many communist states in the 20th century."],["Modernism","/movement/modernism","Movement","c. 1870 CE – c. 1945 CE","Modernism was a movement in the arts and thought, emerging in Western culture in the late 19th century and at its height in the early 20th, that rejected tradition and realism for experiment, abstraction and subjective experience."],["Bagan","/work/bagan","Great work","c. 850 CE – present","Bagan, formerly Pagan, is the medieval capital of the Pagan Kingdom on the Irrawaddy in Myanmar, where rulers and their subjects raised thousands of Buddhist temples, pagodas and monasteries between the 11th and 13th centuries, over 2,300 of which survive."],["Oligarchy","/government/oligarchy","Form of government","c. 700 BCE – 1797 CE","Rule by a few, usually the rich or well-born, in their own interest: the constitution of many Greek cities and of merchant republics such as Venice, and today a loose term for the political power of great wealth."]],d=Math.floor(Date.now()/864e5),p=P[d%P.length],a=document.getElementById("hub-pick");if(a){a.href=p[1];var im=document.createElement("img");im.src="/logos/thumbs/"+encodeURIComponent(p[0]).replace(/'/g,"%27")+".png";im.alt="";im.width=64;im.height=64;a.querySelector(".hub-pick-img").appendChild(im);a.querySelector(".hub-pick-k").textContent="Today\u2019s pick \u00b7 "+p[2]+(p[3]?" \u00b7 "+p[3]:"");a.querySelector(".hub-pick-n").textContent=p[0];a.querySelector(".hub-pick-d").textContent=p[4];}var b=document.getElementById("hub-surprise"),P=["/religion/","/ideology/","/movement/","/work/","/government/"];if(b)b.addEventListener("click",function(){function go(){if(typeof SEARCH_PROFILES==="undefined")return;var a=SEARCH_PROFILES.filter(function(r){return P.some(function(p){return r[1].indexOf(p)===0})});if(a.length)location.href=a[Math.floor(Math.random()*a.length)][1]}if(typeof SEARCH_PROFILES!=="undefined")return go();var sc=document.createElement("script");sc.src=(typeof SEARCH_PROFILES_SRC!=="undefined"?SEARCH_PROFILES_SRC:"/search-profiles.js");sc.onload=go;sc.onerror=go;document.head.appendChild(sc)});})();</script>
